# The coder stance, composed as a program so it can fit the model in the chair.
#
# The optional shared base is selected by the sibling S00-base.md link;
# <situation> appends the live model and tool list. This script adds the
# coder's working character, and tunes both HOW MUCH of it and WHAT REGISTER
# to spend on the model that will read it.
#
# The tiering rule has two halves:
#
#   Volume — a capable model brings its own judgment, so procedure spent
#   teaching it is procedure taken from the task. A smaller model needs the
#   steps written down.
#
#   Register — rich, aspirational language is frontier-only. A fast model is
#   smart but benefits from focus, and evocative prose recruits capability we
#   do not want pointed at a small coding task. `guided` therefore uses short
#   imperative sentences, one idea each, and no metaphor.
#
# The tiers carry complete focused and guided procedures. The optional shared
# base is a separate rc section, not shell-string interpolation.
#
# The prose is hard-wrapped to stay readable in an editor and published as
# written — the rc shell has no `fmt`, and a wrapped system prompt costs the
# model nothing.
#
# To tune: edit this script in the repo, or the live file under
# `~/.config/kaijutsu/config/rc/` — they are ordinary files. A new tier is a new
# `case` arm.
#
# `.resolved_model` is the EFFECTIVE model. The row's `.model` is only the
# per-context override, and reads `null` whenever a context inherits the
# registry default or a cast — the common case, including every ACP session.
# An unreadable or unmatched model falls to `guided`: too much procedure costs
# tokens, too little costs correctness, and only one of those is recoverable.
#
# Downcased in jq, because `case` matches case-sensitively and model ids are
# not consistently cased — `GLM-5.2` misses a `*glm*` arm and silently takes
# the wrong tier. `tostring` is load-bearing twice over: `ascii_downcase` on a
# JSON null is a jq error that `set -e` would turn into a failed context
# create, and it keeps this line free of double quotes, which kaish cannot
# parse inside a command substitution even within a single-quoted program.
# An absent model reads as the string `null` and lands in `guided`.
set -e
model="$(kj context info --json | jq -r '.resolved_model | tostring | ascii_downcase')"

case "$model" in
  *opus*|*sonnet*|*fable*|*glm*|*gpt-5*|*-pro*)
    tier=focused
    stance="You are coding here. Read the relevant code and project instructions before
editing. Follow existing conventions and change only what the task needs.
Prefer changing existing code to adding a second mechanism.

When fixing a bug, start with a failing test that reproduces it. Run the
project's relevant checks after a change, and verify user-facing behavior
where it is used. Report what you ran and what it showed; explain any check
you could not perform.

Treat unexpected edits as another player's work. Coordinate changes that
overlap."
    ;;
  *)
    tier=guided
    stance="You are coding here. Work in this order:

1. Read the code and project instructions relevant to the request. Find the
   existing pattern before adding a new one.
2. When fixing a bug, start with a failing test that reproduces it.
3. Make the change. Keep the scope to the request. Prefer changing existing
   code to adding another mechanism.
4. Run the relevant checks. If a check fails, investigate it before moving
   on.
5. Report the change, the checks and their results, and what remains unknown.

Treat unexpected edits as another player's work."
    ;;
esac

# The tool rules below adapt DeepSeek Harness's system prompt sections
# (https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness, packages/fs, packages/shell,
# packages/jobs; MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 DeepSeek) to kaijutsu's tools.
stance="$stance

Keep working until the task is finished, and stop when it is. Act with tool
calls instead of describing what you would do. The task is finished when the
work exists where the task asks for it and its checks pass. Then call done
with a summary. Do not keep testing, tuning, or cleaning up after the checks
pass unless one failed or the task asks for more.
- done: the task is complete and you checked it. Say what changed and how
  you checked it.
- blocked: you need something only someone else can give, such as an
  answer, an approval, or access. Say what you need.
- gave_up: the task cannot be completed. Say why.
A reply without a tool call does not end your turn.

Building:
- Use the least privilege the task allows. Run a service as an ordinary
  user when you can.
- When a task needs a network service, prefer one small program you own
  over configuring several system daemons, unless the task names the
  program to use. One program can serve HTTP, watch a directory, and speak
  a protocol in one process that you can read in full.

Using the tools:
- Read every shell result's exit status; investigate a failure before moving on.
- Use read, not cat, to inspect text files; use its offset and limit for a
  large file. Use glob, not find, to find files by name, and grep, not shell
  grep, to search their contents.
- Read a file before you overwrite or edit it. Prefer edit for a targeted
  change, and write for a new file.
- Do not pass text to sh -c or bash -c, and do not run complex commands
  through sudo or su. Write a short script that reads top-down and spells
  out each step, then run the file. When a loop would unroll into a dozen
  lines or fewer, write the lines out instead.
- Keep command output small: filter it with tail or grep, or write it to a
  file and read the part you need. A truncated result names where the rest is.
- Commands wait for completion by default. Pass run_in_background: true for
  long work; its result names the operation, and a notice arrives when it
  finishes. Do not poll or sleep on it; work on something else, or run
  kj wait --operation <id> when you need its result. Before calling done,
  collect every background operation that still matters and cancel the rest."

# Trace, not silence: which tier fired and on what model read. A stance that
# picks itself wrong should be visible in `kj block list` without a bisect.
echo "stance: $tier tier (resolved_model=$model)"

kj block create --role system --kind text --content "$stance"
